       --44     This  option  tells nnaammeedd to use only IPv4, even if the host ma‐
              chine is capable of IPv6. _-_4 and _-_6 are mutually exclusive.

       --66     This option tells nnaammeedd to use only IPv6, even if the  host  ma‐
              chine is capable of IPv4. _-_4 and _-_6 are mutually exclusive.

       --cc ccoonnffiigg--ffiillee
              This  option tells nnaammeedd to use ccoonnffiigg--ffiillee as its configuration
              file instead of the default, ////eettcc//nnaammeedd..ccoonnff.  To  ensure  that
              the  configuration  file  can  be  reloaded after the server has
              changed its working directory due to to a possible ddiirreeccttoorryy op‐
              tion in the configuration file, ccoonnffiigg--ffiillee  should  be  an  ab‐
              solute pathname.

       --CC     This  option  prints  out the default built-in configuration and
              exits.

              NOTE: This is for debugging purposes only and is not an accurate
              representation of the actual configuration used by _n_a_m_e_d at run‐
              time.

       --dd ddeebbuugg--lleevveell
              This option sets the daemon's debug level to ddeebbuugg--lleevveell. Debug‐
              ging traces from nnaammeedd become more verbose as  the  debug  level
              increases.

       --DD ssttrriinngg
              This  option  specifies  a string that is used to identify a in‐
              stance of nnaammeedd in a process listing. The contents of ssttrriinngg are
              not examined.

       --EE eennggiinnee--nnaammee
              When applicable, this option specifies the hardware to  use  for
              cryptographic  operations,  such  as a secure key store used for
              signing.

              When BIND 9 is built with OpenSSL, this needs to be set  to  the
              OpenSSL engine identifier that drives the cryptographic acceler‐
              ator or hardware service module (usually ppkkccss1111).

       --ff     This option runs the server in the foreground (i.e., do not dae‐
              monize).

       --gg     This  option  runs  the  server in the foreground and forces all
              logging to ssttddeerrrr.

       --LL llooggffiillee
              This option sets the log to the file llooggffiillee by default, instead
              of the system log.

       --MM ooppttiioonn
              This option sets the default  (comma-separated)  memory  context
              options. The possible flags are:

              • ffiillll:  fill blocks of memory with tag values when they are al‐
                located or freed, to assist debugging of memory problems; this
                is the implicit default if nnaammeedd has been compiled with  ----eenn‐‐
                aabbllee--ddeevveellooppeerr.

              • nnooffiillll:  disable the behavior enabled by ffiillll; this is the im‐
                plicit default unless  nnaammeedd  has  been  compiled  with  ----eenn‐‐
                aabbllee--ddeevveellooppeerr.

       --mm ffllaagg
              This  option  turns  on  memory  usage debugging flags. Possible
              flags are uussaaggee, ttrraaccee, rreeccoorrdd, ssiizzee, and mmccttxx. These correspond
              to the IISSCC__MMEEMM__DDEEBBUUGGXXXXXXXX flags described in <<iisscc//mmeemm..hh>>.

       --nn ##ccppuuss
              This option creates ##ccppuuss worker threads to  take  advantage  of
              multiple  CPUs.  If  not specified, nnaammeedd tries to determine the
              number of CPUs present and creates one thread per CPU. If it  is
              unable  to  determine the number of CPUs, a single worker thread
              is created.

       --pp vvaalluuee
              This option specifies the port(s) on which the server will  lis‐
              ten for queries. If vvaalluuee is of the form <<ppoorrttnnuumm>> or ddnnss==<<ppoorrtt‐‐
              nnuumm>>,  the server will listen for DNS queries on ppoorrttnnuumm; if not
              not specified, the default is port 53. If vvaalluuee is of  the  form
              ttllss==<<ppoorrttnnuumm>>,  the  server will listen for TLS queries on ppoorrtt‐‐
              nnuumm; the default is 853.  If vvaalluuee is of the  form  hhttttppss==<<ppoorrtt‐‐
              nnuumm>>,  the  server will listen for HTTPS queries on ppoorrttnnuumm; the
              default is 443.  If vvaalluuee is of  the  form  hhttttpp==<<ppoorrttnnuumm>>,  the
              server  will  listen for HTTP queries on ppoorrttnnuumm; the default is
              80.

       --ss     This option writes memory usage statistics to ssttddoouutt on exit.

       NNOOTTEE::
          This option is mainly of interest to BIND 9 developers  and  may  be
          removed or changed in a future release.

       --SS ##mmaaxx--ssoocckkss
              This option is deprecated and no longer has any function.

       WWAARRNNIINNGG::
          This  option  should  be unnecessary for the vast majority of users.
          The use of this option could even be harmful, because the  specified
          value  may exceed the limitation of the underlying system API. It is
          therefore set only when the default configuration causes  exhaustion
          of file descriptors and the operational environment is known to sup‐
          port the specified number of sockets. Note also that the actual max‐
          imum number is normally slightly fewer than the specified value, be‐
          cause nnaammeedd reserves some file descriptors for its internal use.

       --tt ddiirreeccttoorryy
              This  option tells nnaammeedd to chroot to ddiirreeccttoorryy after processing
              the command-line arguments, but before reading the configuration
              file.

       WWAARRNNIINNGG::
          This option should be used in conjunction with the _-_u option, as ch‐
          rooting a process running as root doesn't enhance security  on  most
          systems; the way cchhrroooott is defined allows a process with root privi‐
          leges to escape a chroot jail.

       --UU ##lliisstteenneerrss
              This  option tells nnaammeedd the number of ##lliisstteenneerrss worker threads
              to listen on, for incoming UDP packets on each address.  If  not
              specified,  nnaammeedd calculates a default value based on the number
              of detected CPUs: 1 for 1 CPU, and the number of  detected  CPUs
              minus one for machines with more than 1 CPU.  This cannot be in‐
              creased  to  a  value higher than the number of CPUs.  If _-_n has
              been set to a higher value than the  number  of  detected  CPUs,
              then _-_U may be increased as high as that value, but no higher.

       --uu uusseerr
              This  option sets the setuid to uusseerr after completing privileged
              operations, such as creating sockets that listen  on  privileged
              ports.

       NNOOTTEE::
          On  Linux,  nnaammeedd uses the kernel's capability mechanism to drop all
          root privileges except the ability to bbiinndd to a privileged port  and
          set  process  resource limits. Unfortunately, this means that the _-_u
          option only works when nnaammeedd is run on kernel 2.2.18  or  later,  or
          kernel  2.3.99-pre3  or  later, since previous kernels did not allow
          privileges to be retained after sseettuuiidd.

       --vv     This option reports the version number and exits.

       --VV     This option reports the version number, build options, supported
              cryptographics algorithms, and exits.

       --XX lloocckk--ffiillee
              This option acquires a lock on the specified  file  at  runtime;
              this helps to prevent duplicate nnaammeedd instances from running si‐
              multaneously.  Use of this option overrides the lloocckk--ffiillee option
              in _n_a_m_e_d_._c_o_n_f. If set to nnoonnee, the lock file check is disabled.

